# Running the Application Locally

## Clone the repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-github-username>/medicine-interaction-checker.git
```

## Navigate into the project directory

```bash
cd medicine-interaction-checker/medication/medication_checker
```

## Install dependencies

```bash
pip install requests
```

## Run the application

```bash
python3 app.py
```

## Open in your browser

```
http://localhost:8000
```

---

# Deployment

The application was deployed on three Ubuntu servers.

## Web01

The following steps were completed:

1. Cloned the GitHub repository.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Nzizadenilson/medicine-interaction-checker.git
```

2. Navigated into the project directory.

```bash
cd medicine-interaction-checker/medication/medication_checker
```

3. Installed the required dependency.

```bash
pip install requests
```

4. Started the application as a background process.

```bash
nohup python3 app.py > app.log 2>&1 &
```

5. Configured Nginx as a reverse proxy to forward requests to the Python application running on port 8000.

Example configuration:

```nginx
location / {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;

    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
}
```

An additional response header was added:

```nginx
add_header X-Served-By $hostname;
```

This makes it possible to identify which backend server handled a request.

---

## Web02

The same deployment process used for Web01 was repeated:

- Clone the repository
- Install dependencies
- Run the application using `nohup`
- Configure Nginx as a reverse proxy

---

## Load Balancer (Lb01)

HAProxy was configured to distribute requests between both web servers using the **Round Robin** load balancing algorithm.

Example backend configuration:

```haproxy
backend web_back
    balance roundrobin

    server web-01 100.26.186.180:80 check
    server web-02 18.212.74.71:80 check
```

Incoming requests are automatically forwarded to one of the two backend servers, improving reliability and availability.

---

# Deployment Verification

The deployment was verified by:

- Testing the application locally.
- Accessing both web servers individually.
- Accessing the application through the HAProxy load balancer.
- Searching for multiple medicines to verify correct API responses.
- Confirming that requests were forwarded correctly using the `X-Served-By` response header.
- Testing the filtering feature to ensure only the selected information is displayed.

---

# Challenges Encountered

During the development and deployment of this project, I encountered several challenges:

- Understanding the structure of the OpenFDA API responses.
- Some medicines could not be found because they are not available in the OpenFDA database. To address this, I implemented error handling to inform users when no information was available.
- Handling situations where the OpenFDA API could not be reached by displaying an appropriate error message instead of crashing the application.
- Running the application in the background so that it continued running after disconnecting from the server using `nohup`.
- Implementing client-side filtering so users could choose to display only specific medicine information such as warnings, dosage, description, or instructions.

These challenges were resolved through debugging, testing each component individually, and validating the deployment before integrating the complete system.

